// Frozen neutral examples, authored without biographies or matching results.
export const explorationExamples={"animation-interest-044-1":{"id":"animation-interest-044-1","scene":"A drawn creature needs to look surprised, then return to what it was doing. Its shapes must change across several images so the change feels like one moving character.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: In your head, picture three versions of a simple face: before, during, and after a surprise. Consider which shapes you would change and which you would keep recognizable.","reflection":"Would you want to explore making images become moving characters or effects, rather than only looking at finished images? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying a finished animation does not establish interest in repeatedly making its individual images."},"animation-interest-044-2":{"id":"animation-interest-044-2","scene":"A short moving scene shows a character notice a rolling ball and step aside. Viewers keep missing the moment when the character notices it, so the timing and movement need revision.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the same scene with a long pause before the step, then with no pause. Consider which movement or timing you would revise to make the action clear.","reflection":"Would you want to explore planning a moving scene and repeatedly revising its timing or appearance? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This imagined scene does not reveal how you would feel about many rounds of small revisions."},"degree-27101300-047-domain":{"id":"degree-27101300-047-domain","scene":"Two people want to express the feeling of a crowded room without explaining it in words. One uses tightly packed shapes; another leaves a single small figure surrounded by blank space.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Choose an ordinary feeling or observation, such as waiting or a noisy afternoon. Imagine how a shape, color, or material could express it without a caption.","reflection":"Would you want to explore expressing your own ideas through original visual work and its materials? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Having an idea for an image does not establish sustained interest in making original artwork."},"degree-27101300-047-method":{"id":"degree-27101300-047-method","scene":"A picture is meant to feel unsettled, but its neat arrangement makes it feel calm. Its maker tries different spacing, colors, and surfaces, comparing each change with the intended effect.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Picture three identical shapes in a neat row, then unevenly spaced and overlapping. Consider what effect changes and which version could serve an intention you choose.","reflection":"Would you want to explore experimenting with visual choices and revising them toward an artistic intention? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A quick preference between arrangements does not show whether repeated material experimentation would appeal to you."},"degree-27101300-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-27101300-047-responsibility","scene":"Someone has made several images about the same theme. They must decide which belong together, which need finishing, and how their order will affect what viewers understand.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine three pieces about change: a growing plant, a worn object, and a rearranged room. Consider a shared intention, a display order, and one piece you might leave out.","reflection":"Would you want to explore developing a connected body of your own artwork and deciding how to present it? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Choosing a display order is only a small part of sustaining a personal body of work."},"degree-25401300-047-domain":{"id":"degree-25401300-047-domain","scene":"An old illustrated notebook has fragile pages and marks that help explain how it was used. Keeping it available for future study means preserving those details as well as its appearance.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a torn page with faded writing and a stain from its original use. Consider which features future viewers might need preserved rather than cleaned away.","reflection":"Would you want to explore preserving the original materials and meaningful features of objects for future study? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Valuing an old object does not establish interest in the careful material work of preserving it."},"degree-25401300-047-method":{"id":"degree-25401300-047-method","scene":"A painted object has a loose flake and an old repair. A proposed treatment might stabilize it but could also change the original surface, so its condition and options must be examined first.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Without touching or treating anything, imagine choosing between leaving a fragile surface protected in storage or applying a treatment that may change it. Name information you would need before deciding.","reflection":"Would you want to explore examining an object and weighing treatment choices that could alter its original material? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This thought experiment cannot teach a safe treatment or establish competence to carry one out."},"degree-25401300-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-25401300-047-responsibility","scene":"A delicate object is moving into storage. Future handlers will need a record of its condition, any work done, and instructions that protect it without unnecessary alterations.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine writing three headings for that record: current condition, changes made, and handling needs. Think of one detail that would belong under each for a fictional paper object.","reflection":"Would you want to explore careful preservation records and storage or handling recommendations? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Making a short record does not show whether long-term documentation and prevention work would interest you."},"interpreting-interest-044-1":{"id":"interpreting-interest-044-1","scene":"A person gives a spoken or signed message to someone who uses another language. The message needs to cross languages accurately, without adding advice or leaving out an awkward detail.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional message: “I can attend Tuesday only if the time changes.” Restate it in simpler words without changing its condition; no second-language knowledge is needed.","reflection":"Would you want to explore learning to convey spoken or signed messages accurately between languages? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Rephrasing in one language does not test language proficiency or the demands of interpreting in real time."},"interpreting-interest-044-2":{"id":"interpreting-interest-044-2","scene":"Someone says “That was interesting” with a doubtful tone. A word-for-word version in another language might sound enthusiastic, so context and intended tone need attention.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine that phrase said with delight and then with doubt. Consider what context you would ask about before choosing wording that preserves the intended message.","reflection":"Would you want to explore working out meaning and tone across languages, including ambiguous messages? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Noticing tone here does not establish the language knowledge or sustained concentration the full activity requires."},"degree-27102700-047-domain":{"id":"degree-27102700-047-domain","scene":"A small room must help visitors understand a story about a journey. The arrangement of objects, paths, surfaces, and backgrounds will shape how people experience the idea.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine an empty room containing three fictional objects from a journey. Decide where you might place them and what visitors should notice first.","reflection":"Would you want to explore creating physical settings or displays that communicate a story or idea? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying decorating a room does not by itself establish interest in designing settings for someone else’s purpose."},"degree-27102700-047-method":{"id":"degree-27102700-047-method","scene":"A story calls for a setting that changes from a busy square to a quiet room. The available space and materials are limited, so sketches and small models must help test workable arrangements.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Picture a rectangle as the available space. Imagine two arrangements that communicate the change using the same three movable objects, then consider what might need revision.","reflection":"Would you want to explore turning a story or display purpose into spatial plans and revising them around practical limits? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A mental layout does not show how you would feel about detailed drawings, models, or repeated production changes."},"degree-27102700-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-27102700-047-responsibility","scene":"A display team likes a large central structure, but the people installing it need room to move and the materials exceed the budget. The design must be coordinated with everyone who will build and use it.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine keeping either the structure’s size or its costly surface, but not both. Think of questions you would ask the building team before proposing a revision.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating a setting’s design with others while managing space, materials, and budget limits? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Finding one compromise does not establish interest in ongoing coordination through an entire production."},"degree-25112100-047-domain":{"id":"degree-25112100-047-domain","scene":"A group of adult beginners is making short pieces of music, drawings, or performances. They want help developing their own expression and understanding why different creative choices have different effects.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Choose a fictional adult beginner who wants to express excitement. Imagine one question that helps them consider a creative choice instead of simply copying your preference.","reflection":"Would you want to explore helping adult learners develop artistic expression and understand their creative decisions? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying your own creative activity does not establish interest in teaching adults to develop theirs."},"degree-25112100-047-method":{"id":"degree-25112100-047-method","scene":"Adult learners have watched a demonstration but need a way to practice, compare results, and reflect. The next activity must make a technique understandable while leaving room for their own decisions.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine teaching how spacing changes a simple drawing. Plan a tiny demonstration, one practice variation, and a question that lets the learner reflect on the effect.","reflection":"Would you want to explore demonstrating creative techniques and designing practice that helps adult students improve? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A single practice idea does not show how you would adapt teaching to different learners over time."},"degree-25112100-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-25112100-047-responsibility","scene":"An adult learner shares a piece that does not match your personal taste. Useful feedback needs to consider their intention, explain what is working, and suggest a next step they can try.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a piece intended to feel energetic but described by viewers as still. Form a feedback sentence about one observable choice and a possible experiment, without judging the person.","reflection":"Would you want to explore evaluating creative work and giving constructive feedback that supports another person’s development? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Being kind about one piece does not establish interest in repeated, fair evaluation of students’ work."},"degree-15129903-047-domain":{"id":"degree-15129903-047-domain","scene":"A community group has years of electronic forms, plans, and messages. People need to find the current version, know who may use it, and avoid keeping every file forever.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine five fictional files: an old plan, a current plan, a public notice, a private form, and a duplicate. Consider how each should be stored, found, shared, or eventually removed.","reflection":"Would you want to explore organizing the full life of electronic documents and records? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Keeping your own files tidy does not establish interest in managing shared records across an organization."},"degree-15129903-047-method":{"id":"degree-15129903-047-method","scene":"People search for the same records in different ways: by date, project, or document type. A useful system needs categories and steps based on those needs, rather than one person’s filing habits.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine three fictional groups looking for meeting records: people seeking dates, decisions, and project history. Propose two labels or retrieval steps that would help more than one group.","reflection":"Would you want to explore comparing documents and users’ needs to design ways of finding and managing information? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Inventing two labels does not show how you would test a system across many users and document types."},"degree-15129903-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-15129903-047-responsibility","scene":"A shared record is useful to several teams but contains private information and must be kept for a set period. Access, retention, and eventual secure disposal need rules that fit together.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Using a fictional record only, imagine deciding who needs the full record, who needs a limited version, and what you must learn before setting a deletion date.","reflection":"Would you want to explore balancing useful access, confidentiality, retention duties, and secure disposal? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This example does not provide legal retention rules or establish readiness to handle confidential records."},"degree-11301200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-11301200-047-domain","scene":"A small organization keeps losing time because supplies run out, records are hard to find, and service agreements are unclear. Its everyday support work needs to fit together reliably.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a shared room used by several groups. Identify one supply, one record, and one outside service that someone would need to organize so the room keeps working.","reflection":"Would you want to explore organizing records, supplies, agreements, and services that support other people’s everyday work? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Organizing one room does not establish interest in managing many continuing support services."},"degree-11301200-047-method":{"id":"degree-11301200-047-method","scene":"Two departments order the same supplies separately: one gets deliveries quickly but pays more, while the other spends time chasing missing items. Their workflows and costs need comparison before changing the service.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine one process with frequent small orders and another with a shared monthly order. Name a cost and a reliability question you would compare before choosing.","reflection":"Would you want to explore comparing support workflows and costs to work out where shared services need improvement? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A simple comparison does not show how you would investigate competing needs across a whole organization."},"degree-11301200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-11301200-047-responsibility","scene":"Several departments need reliable help from a small support team. Someone must organize staff responsibilities and the service budget while checking that essential requests are actually completed.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine three requests arriving together: a missing essential supply, a routine record update, and a room booking next month. Consider what you would ask before assigning staff time and funds.","reflection":"Would you want to explore overseeing support staff and budgets while being accountable for reliable help? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Choosing an order for three requests does not establish interest in ongoing staff supervision and budget responsibility."},"degree-23102200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-23102200-047-domain","scene":"Two groups disagree about how a shared room was promised to them. Each has concerns and believes the rules support its position; a structured process is needed to address the dispute.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine each group gets one uninterrupted turn to explain its concern. Consider one question that would help you understand both sides before suggesting any outcome.","reflection":"Would you want to explore helping opposing parties work through disputes using a structured process and relevant rules? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Wanting people to get along does not establish interest in sustained conflict-resolution work."},"degree-23102200-047-method":{"id":"degree-23102200-047-method","scene":"In a disagreement over a shared agreement, the two accounts do not line up. Messages and dates may support some details, while other details remain uncertain.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine one account says a change was agreed and another says it was only suggested. Identify a piece of evidence you would seek and two possible explanations to keep open.","reflection":"Would you want to explore listening to competing accounts, examining evidence, and identifying resolutions within an agreed process? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A fictional disagreement does not reproduce the pressure or complexity of examining real disputed evidence."},"degree-23102200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-23102200-047-responsibility","scene":"One dispute process allows only a voluntary agreement; another authorizes a decision. Participants need fair opportunities to be heard and a clear record of what the process actually produced.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine writing the outcome of a fictional disagreement. Consider what you would record differently if both sides agreed versus a designated person made a decision.","reflection":"Would you want to explore conducting fair proceedings and accurately documenting an agreement or authorized decision? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Knowing these two outcomes differ does not establish readiness to conduct a formal proceeding."},"degree-19309200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-19309200-047-domain","scene":"Two nearby neighborhoods experience different summer temperatures. Trees, building surfaces, traffic, and how people use the land might help explain the pattern together.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Picture two imaginary neighborhoods, one shaded and one mostly paved. Think of a natural feature and a human activity you would investigate before explaining their temperature difference.","reflection":"Would you want to explore how natural features and human activity interact to create patterns between places? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Being interested in one place does not establish interest in systematic geographic investigation."},"degree-19309200-047-method":{"id":"degree-19309200-047-method","scene":"A map shows that some areas have much longer journeys to a public space than others. Maps alone cannot explain the difference; observations and information about the people living there are also needed.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine comparing a map of paths, notes about barriers, and counts of nearby residents. Consider how using all three could change an explanation based only on distance.","reflection":"Would you want to explore combining maps, observations, and population information to explain geographic patterns? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This example does not test map skills or show whether gathering and analyzing multiple sources would appeal over time."},"degree-19309200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-19309200-047-responsibility","scene":"A report argues that changes in one area affected travel in a neighboring area. Its readers need to understand the evidence, alternative explanations, and where the information is incomplete.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine explaining a fictional pattern using the sentence “This may be related to…, but we do not yet know…”. Identify evidence you would need to make the explanation stronger.","reflection":"Would you want to explore communicating evidence-based relationships between places, including what the evidence cannot establish? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A cautious sentence does not establish interest in developing and defending a full research explanation."},"biological-technician-interest-044-1":{"id":"biological-technician-interest-044-1","scene":"A research team compares plant material from several locations. Each sample must be collected and prepared consistently so a mix-up or difference in handling does not distort the study.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Without collecting anything, imagine three fictional plant samples from different locations. Decide what labels and preparation notes would help a team compare them fairly.","reflection":"Would you want to explore carefully collecting and preparing biological samples to support the study of living things? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Liking plants or animals does not establish interest in repeated sample preparation and handling procedures."},"biological-technician-interest-044-2":{"id":"biological-technician-interest-044-2","scene":"A study compares how seeds develop under two defined conditions. Following the procedure, observing consistently, and recording unexpected results matter as much as the hoped-for outcome.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: In your head, plan a fictional record for two groups of seeds. Name one thing to keep the same, one observation to record, and how to note a result that surprises you; do not run an experiment.","reflection":"Would you want to explore carrying out biological experiments carefully and recording observations for a research team? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This imagined record does not test experimental skills or establish interest in the repetition of research procedures."},"degree-43911100-047-domain":{"id":"degree-43911100-047-domain","scene":"A study team has collected fictional counts from several groups. It needs someone to organize them and follow a specified calculation procedure consistently so its summaries can be trusted.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the instructions say to add each group’s counts, then divide by the number of observations. Consider how you would organize and check that process; no calculation is required.","reflection":"Would you want to explore supporting studies through organized information and accurate use of established statistical procedures? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying a number puzzle does not establish interest in following study procedures accurately across many records."},"degree-43911100-047-method":{"id":"degree-43911100-047-method","scene":"A table mixes missing responses with actual zeros. Applying the requested calculation without checking those entries could produce a misleading summary.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine three fictional entries: 0, 4, and “not answered.” Consider what you would clarify before calculating and how you might label a table so readers see the difference.","reflection":"Would you want to explore checking data, applying specified calculations, and preparing useful study tables or charts? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Spotting one data issue does not establish statistical competence or interest in extended data preparation."},"degree-43911100-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-43911100-047-responsibility","scene":"A study summary has been revised several times. The team needs reliable records of which figures were used, how results were calculated, and which uncertainties require further review.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a total changes after a missing record arrives. Think of a note that would explain the change and a question you would send back to the team rather than answer without evidence.","reflection":"Would you want to explore maintaining study records, explaining calculation results, and flagging uncertainty for review? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A short explanation does not show how you would feel about ongoing accuracy and record-maintenance responsibilities."},"accounting-interest-044-1":{"id":"accounting-interest-044-1","scene":"A fictional organization receives money, pays bills, and has obligations still unpaid. A useful financial report must show these clearly and follow the reporting rules, rather than just list cash in a box.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a group has received a payment but still owes for supplies. Consider what a report should explain so another person understands both the money available and the obligation.","reflection":"Would you want to explore preparing and analyzing financial records and reports under reporting rules? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Keeping a personal spending list is not the same as preparing an organization’s financial reports."},"accounting-interest-044-2":{"id":"accounting-interest-044-2","scene":"A payment appears in one fictional record but not another, so the accounts do not agree. The difference could be timing, a duplicate entry, or a mistake that needs correcting.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a payment marked on a receipt but absent from a ledger. Think of two explanations you would check and what evidence would justify a correction.","reflection":"Would you want to explore tracing differences in accounts and working out what needs correction? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Solving one discrepancy does not establish interest in repeated checking under financial reporting requirements."},"degree-43908100-047-domain":{"id":"degree-43908100-047-domain","scene":"A short notice contains a misspelled name, inconsistent punctuation, and a line break that changes how a sentence reads. These details need checking before people rely on the published version.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the notice “Doors open at 6.30; Bring a ticket.” Identify a consistency question you would check before publication, without assuming one style is always correct.","reflection":"Would you want to explore finding language and layout errors so written material is accurate and consistent before publication? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Noticing an occasional typo does not establish interest in sustained, detailed checking of other people’s writing."},"degree-43908100-047-method":{"id":"degree-43908100-047-method","scene":"A prepared paragraph must match an approved original and a publication’s style requirements. After corrections are requested, the revised version needs another comparison to catch missed or new errors.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine an approved line says “Meet in Room B at noon,” but a draft says “Meet in Room D at noon.” Consider how you would verify both the requested correction and the final version.","reflection":"Would you want to explore comparing text with intended wording and style rules, then checking revisions again? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Finding an obvious difference does not show whether repeated, close comparison of long texts would appeal to you."},"degree-43908100-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-43908100-047-responsibility","scene":"A publication deadline is near, and a correction could accidentally change the writer’s meaning. The final check needs clear notes about what to change and what must be clarified first.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the phrase “only visitors with passes.” Think of a question you would ask before moving the word “only,” and how you would record a confirmed correction unambiguously.","reflection":"Would you want to explore taking responsibility for clear correction records and careful final checks while preserving intended meaning? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A brief wording question does not reproduce sustained deadline pressure or responsibility for a final publication."},"project-management-interest-044-1":{"id":"project-management-interest-044-1","scene":"A group has agreed to create a small public display by a set date. Its goal still needs to become a plan for tasks, people, timing, and the funds available.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the display needs content, a layout, and installation. Consider a task dependency, a person’s responsibility, a deadline, and a spending limit that would belong in the plan.","reflection":"Would you want to explore turning agreed work goals into a plan for people, tasks, deadlines, and budget? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying making plans for yourself does not establish interest in managing a shared work project."},"project-management-interest-044-2":{"id":"project-management-interest-044-2","scene":"A team’s display is due soon, but one essential part is late. Someone must check progress, coordinate a response, communicate with the recipient, and remain accountable for the agreed result.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the missing part can be simplified or delayed, but either choice affects the agreement. Consider who you would consult and how you would explain the effect before changing the plan.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating a team through problems and being accountable for delivering agreed results? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Offering one solution does not show how you would feel about continuous coordination and delivery responsibility."},"logistics-interest-044-1":{"id":"logistics-interest-044-1","scene":"A group needs different supplies at three locations before activities begin. Ordering enough is only one part of the problem: timing, storage, transport, and destination all need to line up.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine boxes needed at two fictional sites on different days. Consider what information about arrival time, storage, and delivery order you would need before making a plan.","reflection":"Would you want to explore planning how supplies or products reach the right place at the right time? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Planning one delivery does not establish interest in ongoing organizational supply and distribution work."},"logistics-interest-044-2":{"id":"logistics-interest-044-2","scene":"A supplier reports a delay while a customer still expects a delivery. Transport arrangements, available stock, and the customer’s priorities must be checked before making a revised commitment.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine the delayed item can arrive tomorrow or a substitute can arrive today. Consider what you would ask the supplier, transporter, and recipient before arranging the change.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating suppliers, transport, and customers when delivery plans go wrong? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A fictional delay does not reveal how repeated disruptions and competing commitments would feel to you."},"degree-13112100-047-domain":{"id":"degree-13112100-047-domain","scene":"An organized gathering is meant to help participants exchange ideas, but its format is still undecided. The purpose needs to guide how people arrive, meet, participate, and leave.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a small gathering whose aim is for strangers to share ideas. Choose an arrangement that supports that purpose and one participant need you would ask about.","reflection":"Would you want to explore turning the purpose of an organized event into a workable participant experience? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying attending gatherings does not establish interest in planning the experience for other participants."},"degree-13112100-047-method":{"id":"degree-13112100-047-method","scene":"A planned gathering has a room, a schedule, outside suppliers, and participants with different needs. A room change means the arrangements must be coordinated again rather than changed in isolation.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional room becomes unavailable. Consider which schedule, supplier, and participant details you would recheck before choosing another space.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating event arrangements and adjusting them when requirements change? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Finding a replacement room is only a small part of managing changing event logistics."},"degree-13112100-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-13112100-047-responsibility","scene":"Several suppliers have promised parts of a gathering, and bills arrive with different terms. Someone must track commitments, check delivery, and resolve differences from what was promised.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a bill includes an extra item the client did not approve. Think of the agreement and delivery information you would check before accepting or disputing it.","reflection":"Would you want to explore managing event commitments and bills while being accountable for promised arrangements? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Checking one bill does not establish interest in financial and service accountability across an entire event."},"degree-13202200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-13202200-047-domain","scene":"Two objects look similar but may have different values because of condition, history, and the purpose of the estimate. A transaction needs a reasoned value rather than a guess from appearance.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine two fictional secondhand desks, one damaged and one restored. Name information you would need, including the reason for valuing them, before estimating either one.","reflection":"Would you want to explore investigating the value of belongings, equipment, artwork, or business property for a specific decision? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying shopping or collecting does not establish interest in producing careful valuations for others."},"degree-13202200-047-method":{"id":"degree-13202200-047-method","scene":"An item’s apparent value differs from recent comparable items. Its condition, features, and the quality of the comparison evidence need examination before an estimate is justified.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional used table with a repaired leg and unusual dimensions. Consider which differences from comparison items you would investigate rather than immediately adding or subtracting value.","reflection":"Would you want to explore examining items and comparing evidence to develop a reasoned value estimate? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This comparison cannot establish appraisal skill or a reliable value for any real item."},"degree-13202200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-13202200-047-responsibility","scene":"A person will use a valuation in an important financial decision. They need a clear record of the evidence, assumptions, purpose, and uncertainty behind the estimate.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine explaining a fictional estimate based on a limited condition description. Name an assumption you would disclose and a limitation that a reader should understand before relying on it.","reflection":"Would you want to explore documenting valuations and explaining their assumptions to people who rely on them? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Stating one limitation does not establish readiness to take responsibility for consequential valuations."},"degree-17214102-047-domain":{"id":"degree-17214102-047-domain","scene":"A vehicle is being redesigned to use energy more efficiently while carrying the same load. The work concerns how its structure, power, and movement fit together, rather than repairing a broken part.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional vehicle whose new body is lighter but has less space for its power system. Consider how changing one system could affect another; no technical calculation is needed.","reflection":"Would you want to explore developing new or improved vehicle systems, including how their parts interact? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Liking driving or repairing vehicles does not establish interest in developing their underlying systems."},"degree-17214102-047-method":{"id":"degree-17214102-047-method","scene":"A vehicle design performs well in one test but poorly in another. Measurements from individual components and the whole system are needed to investigate the failure and compare possible design changes.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional vehicle that works well on a smooth route but loses performance on a rough one. Identify two measurements you would want before deciding what to redesign; do not test a real vehicle.","reflection":"Would you want to explore testing vehicle systems, investigating failures, and comparing design improvements? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This scenario does not test technical skill or establish whether extended testing and analysis would interest you."},"degree-17214102-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-17214102-047-responsibility","scene":"A proposed vehicle change improves one feature but may affect reliability and other systems. Specialists must coordinate the change and check the whole vehicle against its safety and performance requirements.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional design change creates more storage space but moves a heavy component. Consider which other specialists and whole-system checks would need to be involved before approval.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating vehicle design changes and checking their effects on safety, reliability, and overall requirements? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Identifying a tradeoff does not establish competence to approve a safety-critical design."},"degree-27102200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-27102200-047-domain","scene":"A wearable item is meant to communicate a particular look while fitting a moving person and being practical to make. Appearance, material, fit, and construction all shape the idea.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional jacket intended to feel flowing while allowing easy arm movement. Consider one material choice and one shape choice you would want to explore together.","reflection":"Would you want to explore creating clothing or accessories by combining appearance, materials, fit, and how they are made? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying wearing or choosing clothes does not establish interest in developing their designs."},"degree-27102200-047-method":{"id":"degree-27102200-047-method","scene":"A first sample looks right while still but pulls awkwardly during movement. Sketches, materials, and construction choices need revision after examining the fit and visual effect.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional sleeve that restricts reaching. Think of two changes you might compare and what you would examine in another sample; no sewing or materials are required.","reflection":"Would you want to explore developing and revising wearable samples after comparing materials, construction, fit, and effect? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A proposed adjustment does not show whether repeated sampling and fit revision would appeal to you."},"degree-27102200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-27102200-047-responsibility","scene":"A design must be made by people who did not create the original idea. They need clear details about materials, fit, and construction, including how to handle practical limits without losing the intended look.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine someone else must make a fictional bag from your description. Name a measurement, a material detail, and an intended effect you would explain so they do not have to guess.","reflection":"Would you want to explore communicating production details and balancing an intended look with practical making constraints? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Describing one object does not establish interest in sustained production communication and revisions."},"degree-29114101-047-domain":{"id":"degree-29114101-047-domain","scene":"A fictional patient is seriously ill, and their condition could worsen quickly. The activity involves learning to assess changes closely and participate in prompt treatment, alongside the person receiving care.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Without giving advice or choosing a treatment, imagine the difference between a stable routine visit and close care where important changes may happen rapidly. Consider which aspects you would want explained further.","reflection":"Would you want to explore learning to care for people whose serious illness or injury needs close assessment and prompt treatment? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"An imagined situation cannot predict your response to clinical urgency or establish clinical ability."},"degree-29114101-047-method":{"id":"degree-29114101-047-method","scene":"In a fictional care setting, several observations about a seriously ill person are changing. A trained team must interpret those findings, recognize instability, and decide what care is appropriate within each person’s role.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Using no real symptoms or medical decisions, imagine a timeline with several changing observations. Consider whether you would want to learn how trained people distinguish important changes from uncertain ones.","reflection":"Would you want to explore learning to interpret clinical changes and choose appropriate immediate care within a defined role? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This miniature supplies no diagnostic guidance and cannot test readiness to make clinical decisions."},"degree-29114101-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-29114101-047-responsibility","scene":"A seriously ill person’s care involves the patient, family, and several clinicians. Changes in response need monitoring, clear communication, and timely escalation when the condition is threatened.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional team update with headings for what changed, what was done, and who needs to know. Consider whether coordinating that information during urgent care is something you would want to understand further.","reflection":"Would you want to explore coordinating urgent care, monitoring responses, and escalating concerning changes with patients, families, and clinicians? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A communication outline does not establish comfort with the responsibility or emotional demands of urgent care."},"degree-29114103-047-domain":{"id":"degree-29114103-047-domain","scene":"A fictional patient is severely ill and needs continuous attention because breathing, circulation, or other vital functions may change quickly. Care focuses on the person as well as those changing functions.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Without interpreting any real measurements, imagine returning your attention repeatedly to the same severely ill person over a shift. Consider what you would want to learn about that form of care.","reflection":"Would you want to explore caring for severely ill people whose vital functions need close and continuing attention? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"General concern for others does not establish interest in continuous care of critically ill patients."},"degree-29114103-047-method":{"id":"degree-29114103-047-method","scene":"A fictional patient’s measurements and test results form a changing picture. Trained caregivers compare information over time to recognize deterioration and work out the immediate care needed.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a page of fictional observations labeled earlier, now, and uncertain, with no medical values. Think about whether learning to connect changes across these sources interests you in this clinical setting.","reflection":"Would you want to explore interpreting changing patient measurements and test results to identify deterioration and immediate care needs? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This exercise teaches no interpretation of medical values and cannot establish clinical decision-making ability."},"degree-29114103-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-29114103-047-responsibility","scene":"Continuous care of a critically ill person includes prescribed treatments, checks on the response, and prompt action when dangerous changes occur. Handoffs must keep the next caregiver fully informed.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine handing over a fictional care record organized into prescribed actions, response, and unresolved concerns. Consider whether you would want to learn about maintaining that responsibility through a continuous care shift.","reflection":"Would you want to explore providing prescribed treatments and coordinating continuous care while responding promptly to dangerous changes? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A record outline does not reproduce treatment responsibility, sustained vigilance, or the demands of a critical condition."},"degree-19302200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-19302200-047-domain","scene":"A group wants to understand how people experience a shared service. A few vivid stories may miss common experiences, so information needs to be gathered systematically from the group.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional group asking whether its meeting times work for members. Consider what you would need to learn from more than the two most vocal people.","reflection":"Would you want to explore systematically gathering information about what a group thinks, experiences, or does? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Being curious about people does not establish interest in systematic survey research."},"degree-19302200-047-method":{"id":"degree-19302200-047-method","scene":"A draft question asks whether a service is “quick and helpful,” but someone may find it quick and unhelpful. Who receives the question also affects what the answers can tell the team.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Think of how you might separate those two ideas, then name a group of users who could be missed if the question appeared only at one time or place.","reflection":"Would you want to explore testing question wording and sampling approaches to reduce misunderstanding and improve a study? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Improving one question does not establish survey-design skill or interest in repeated testing and revision."},"degree-19302200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-19302200-047-responsibility","scene":"A survey receives positive responses, but many people never had a chance to answer. Its report must explain whose experiences appear in the findings and what conclusions would go beyond the responses.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine that only morning visitors answered a fictional service survey. Form one finding you could report and one broader claim you would hold back.","reflection":"Would you want to explore explaining survey findings and clearly describing missing experiences and limits on conclusions? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Identifying one limitation does not establish interest in carefully interpreting and communicating a complete survey study."},"degree-13209904-047-domain":{"id":"degree-13209904-047-domain","scene":"An organization suspects that money or property has been misrepresented. A discrepancy could reflect deception, a mistake, or an incomplete record, so the question needs investigation rather than an immediate accusation.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional item listed as purchased but not found in storage. Identify two explanations besides deliberate wrongdoing that evidence would need to distinguish.","reflection":"Would you want to explore investigating suspected deception involving money or property by working from evidence? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying a mystery does not establish interest in careful financial or property investigations."},"degree-13209904-047-method":{"id":"degree-13209904-047-method","scene":"Fictional records, statements, and activity patterns seem to conflict. Each possible explanation must be tested against what the evidence supports and what information is still missing.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine an invoice date differs from a delivery account. Consider what additional record would help test whether this is a timing difference, an error, or possible deception.","reflection":"Would you want to explore comparing records and statements to test explanations and identify missing evidence in suspected fraud? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"A short discrepancy does not reproduce the volume, ambiguity, or persistence of a full investigation."},"degree-13209904-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-13209904-047-responsibility","scene":"An investigation’s conclusions may be challenged or used in proceedings. Readers need to trace the evidence, distinguish facts from interpretations, and see the limits of what was established.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine writing a fictional finding with separate lines for observed fact, possible explanation, and evidence still needed. Consider how you would avoid presenting a suspicion as a proven conclusion.","reflection":"Would you want to explore preserving investigation records and explaining findings accurately when they may be formally challenged? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This record exercise does not establish readiness to conduct legal proceedings or make an accusation."},"degree-19201100-047-domain":{"id":"degree-19201100-047-domain","scene":"Light from distant objects contains clues about places no one can visit directly. Observations of stars, planets, and galaxies raise questions about how the universe works and changes.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine distant lights whose brightness changes over time. Choose a question about the objects themselves that you would want to investigate, rather than simply admire their appearance.","reflection":"Would you want to explore investigating phenomena beyond Earth to understand how the universe works? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying space images or stories does not establish interest in systematic research about astronomical phenomena."},"degree-19201100-047-method":{"id":"degree-19201100-047-method","scene":"A telescope record shows a repeating change in brightness. Several explanations might fit, so observations and scientific models must be compared rather than choosing the most exciting story.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a light becoming dimmer at regular intervals. Without needing scientific knowledge, name an observation you would want to compare across cycles before deciding which explanation fits.","reflection":"Would you want to explore analyzing telescope observations and comparing explanations for patterns in light or motion in space? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This imagined pattern does not test the mathematics or sustained data analysis involved in the full activity."},"degree-19201100-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-19201100-047-responsibility","scene":"A team can observe a distant object only at certain times and with limited precision. Its investigation and report must make the question, evidence, uncertainty, and observation limits clear.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional object was observed on only three nights. Consider a question the observations might address and a claim you would avoid making without more data.","reflection":"Would you want to explore designing and communicating a defensible investigation of a question about space, including uncertainty? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Naming a limitation does not establish interest in planning and defending a full astronomical investigation."},"degree-19201200-047-domain":{"id":"degree-19201200-047-domain","scene":"An everyday physical effect behaves differently than expected, and the existing explanation is incomplete. Investigating it means asking about underlying principles of matter, energy, motion, or light.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine a fictional material that changes how light passes through it when its temperature changes. Think of a question about the underlying physical explanation you would want to pursue; do not test anything.","reflection":"Would you want to explore investigating physical principles to explain phenomena that are not yet understood? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Enjoying an interesting physical effect does not establish interest in developing a scientific explanation for it."},"degree-19201200-047-method":{"id":"degree-19201200-047-method","scene":"Two mathematical models predict different patterns for the same physical system. Measurements or experiments must be designed and compared with the predictions to test which explanation holds up.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine one model predicts a straight-line pattern and another a curve. Consider where additional measurements might help distinguish them; no calculations or experiment are needed.","reflection":"Would you want to explore combining mathematical models and measurements to test competing explanations of physical behavior? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"This conceptual comparison does not test mathematical skill or interest in extended experimental and modeling work."},"degree-19201200-047-responsibility":{"id":"degree-19201200-047-responsibility","scene":"A physical investigation’s result depends on assumptions and measurements with uncertainty. Other people need enough detail to examine the reasoning and, where possible, repeat the investigation.","tryIt":"Optional, up to two minutes: Imagine describing a fictional measurement to someone who was absent. Think of an assumption, a measurement detail, and an uncertainty they would need before trying to reproduce the result.","reflection":"Would you want to explore reporting physical research clearly enough for others to examine its assumptions and repeat its methods? It is equally okay to choose no, unsure, or unfamiliar.","limit":"Listing three details does not establish interest in the full discipline of documenting and defending research."}};
